# 3️⃣ Inbound vs Outbound – Quick Comparison
                | Aspect        | Outbound                        | Inbound         |
                | ------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------- |
                | Config Object | Named Credential, Auth Provider | Connected App   |
                | OAuth Client  | Salesforce                      | External App    |
                | Token Stored  | Salesforce (Named Credential)   | External App    |
                | Auth Server   | External System                 | Salesforce      |
                | APIs Called   | External APIs                   | Salesforce APIs |

2. client credentials flow - Client Credentials Flow in Salesforce is used for machine-to-machine integration where outbound uses Named Credentials to call external APIs,
                and inbound uses Connected Apps with a Run As User to allow external systems to access Salesforce APIs without user interaction.
                The client authenticates with the authorization server using its client ID and secret to obtain an access token.
                inbound flow - external system calls Salesforce API using access token obtained from connected app's OAuth flow.
                outbound flow - Salesforce calls external API using access token obtained from named credential's OAuth flow. 

            Named credentials with OAuth 2.0 support can be used to set up the authorization code flow for a web application that needs to access
                    an external API on behalf of the user.

            1. User tries to access a protected resource in the client application (e.g., a web app).
            2. The client application redirects the user to the authorization server's authorization endpoint, including parameters such as client_id, redirect_uri, 
                response_type=code, and scope.
            3. The user authenticates with the authorization server and grants permission to the client application.
            4. The authorization server redirects the user back to the specified redirect_uri with an authorization code in the URL parameters.
            5. The client application receives the authorization code and sends a POST request to the authorization server's token endpoint,
                including the authorization code, client_id, client_secret, and redirect_uri to exchange it for an access token.
                                                        

1. Outbound or callouts - The request is made from Salesforce org to an external system. 
   Concepts:
     i. Named Credentials / Remote Site Settings
     ii. External credentials 
     iii Rest API callouts (HTTP methods, authentication, etc.)
2. Inbound or call-ins - The request is made from an external system to Salesforce org. 
   Concepts:
     i.  Connected App
     ii.  Custom Apex REST API (@RestResource)
     iii. Authentication (OAuth, JWT, etc.)   

why we need to set up remote site settings for outbound callouts in Salesforce?
    Salesforce requires you to define Remote Site Settings to specify which external domains your Salesforce org can communicatete with. 
    This is a security measure to prevent unauthorized callouts to external systems. By setting up Remote Site Settings, you explicitly allow Salesforce to
    make HTTP callouts to the specified external URLs.

setup -> remote site settings -> add new remote site -> enter name and url of external system -> save

Named credentials in salesforce
    named credentials is a secure way to store and manage authentication credentials for external systems in Salesforce. It allows you to define the endpoint URL,
    authentication type, and credentials in one place, and then reference it in your Apex code or Flow without hardcoding sensitive information.
    To set up Named Credentials:
    1. Go to Setup → Named Credentials → New Named Credential
    2. Enter the following details:
    - Label: My External API 
    - Name: My_External_API
    - URL: https://api.external-system.com
    - Identity Type: Named Principal (or Per User if you want to use different credentials for each user)
    - Authentication Protocol: Choose the appropriate authentication method (e.g., Password Authentication, OAuth 2.0, etc.)
    - Enter the required credentials based on the chosen authentication protocol (e.g., username, password, client ID, client secret, etc.)

        Rest API Outbound (Salesforce calls external system)
        1. Use HTTP Callouts in Apex to send data to external system.
        call the API from Apex or Flow as needed.
            HttpRequest req=new HttpRequest();
            Http http=new Http();
            req.setEndpoint('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/1');
            req.setMethod('GET');
            req.setHeader('Content-Type','application/json');
            HttpResponse res=http.send(req);
            system.debug('API Response: ' + res.getBody());

        2. You can also call external REST API from Flow using HTTP Callout action.
            HTTP Callout in Flow:
            - Method: POST
